Dear Minister Anand and Minister McGuinty,

We are copying this letter to Minister Lightbound (Public Works and Procurement), Minister Sidhu (International Trade), Secretary of State Fuhr, the Office of the Prime Minister, and the opposition defence critics, as the evidence submitted directly concerns the procedural and statutory framework governing Canada's $60 billion submarine procurement evaluation.

CC note to Dr. Andrew Latham: As with our previous correspondence, your independent technical analysis regarding the KSS-III platform's unsuitability for Canadian Arctic requirements is directly cited in Section II.C of this ministerial submission.

On February 25, 2026, Minister McGuinty stated at the joint press conference following the Korea-Canada two-plus-two meeting in Ottawa:

"Neither Minister Anand and I are in a position to talk about the merits of the specifics of one bid over another. We want to make sure this is arm's length and objective."

"The good news is this is why we stood up under our new government a whole new process to do this fairly and objectively and responsibly."

We write in direct response to that statement.

Gender Watchdog is an advocacy organization documenting institutional integrity failures and racialized sexual violence in higher education, supported by EROC (End Rape On Campus) and acting in coordination with Canadian constituents. We have been submitting documented evidence to Canadian government officials since January 26, 2026 — one month before the Ottawa ministerial meetings that produced the "fair process" pledge.

The evidence we have submitted is not in dispute by any party that has received it. The question raised by Minister McGuinty's statement is: what does a "fair" process mean when the documented record of the company being evaluated has already been placed on the table and no response has been issued?

We are copying Embassy of Canada Seoul on this letter — the diplomatic address that first received our evidence on April 27, 2025. The Seoul embassy is not new to this file.Executive SummaryImminent Risk: Canada is entering a $60B defense partnership with a supply chain currently under forensic audit for systemic fraud (34+ falsified university partnerships) and labor rights violations (Geoje shipyard discrimination).Evidence Ignored: Global Affairs Canada has received documented evidence of these risks since April 2025 but has initiated no internal escalation (confirmed by ATIP EA2025_0134154).Legal Jeopardy: The documented 61.5% sexual violence rate in the partner ecosystem, combined with non-consent rape laws, weaponized defamation statutes that criminalize truth, and a history of state-sponsored prostitution, creates a liability framework comparable to the Epstein network's structure — an argument Gender Watchdog is advancing to the UN Human Rights Council.The Ask: An independent supply chain audit under the Fighting Against Forced Labour and Child Labour in Supply Chains Act before any binding agreement is signed.0. A Ten-Month Diplomatic Record Canada Has Not Acted On

This is not the beginning of our engagement with the Canadian government on Korean institutional integrity. It is the tenth month.

April 27, 2025 — Gender Watchdog sent its first written advisory to the Canadian Embassy in Seoul (seoul@international.gc.ca, seoul-cs@international.gc.ca, seoul-gr@international.gc.ca) documenting:Sexual violence risks in Korean university arts and culture programsUniversity of Manitoba's explicit email denial of any exchange agreement with Dongguk UniversityThe pattern of falsified international partnerships being used by Korean universities to inflate rankings and academic credentials

No response was received from Embassy Seoul.

June 17, 2025 — The Canadian Trade Office in Taipei (CTOT) received a contact from Gender Watchdog involving the same Korean higher education safety concerns. CTOT routed the matter to Embassy Seoul, describing it as a "sensitive matter" — the only substantive diplomatic acknowledgment our advocacy received from any Canadian government entity during this period.

The redacted screenshot of this acknowledgment has been published:

X post (Sep 24, 2025): https://x.com/Gender_Watchdog/status/1959510392465698985

Context blog: https://blog.genderwatchdog.org/traffic-spike-evidence-institutional-monitoring-arms-export-campaigns/

August 16, 2025 — Gender Watchdog filed an Access to Information and Privacy (ATIP) request with Global Affairs Canada (reference number EA2025_0134154), requesting all Seoul embassy communications about Dongguk University allegations (UBC/Manitoba partnership fraud) and the JIFF (Jeonju International Film Festival) connection, covering the scope window of April 10 – September 12, 2025. The request required multiple clarification exchanges over the following months — including a back-and-forth on whether Hangul keywords could be used in the search scope — before the response notification was received on January 5, 2026: nearly five months of processing.

The result: no internal GAC communications existed on this topic except Gender Watchdog's own outbound emails to Seoul embassy. The Canadian government received the evidence — and generated zero internal escalation, policy review, or protective action in response.

Privacy Note: Gender Watchdog requests that Global Affairs Canada treat the identity of the individual who filed ATIP reference EA2025_0134154 as protected information. The filer works directly with survivors of sexual violence in Korean institutional contexts. Korea's criminal defamation law — documented in Section IV.B of this submission — permits prosecution of truthful statements and has already been weaponized against this advocacy by a Korean entertainment conglomerate (Sidus FNH, retained Shinwon law firm). Disclosure of the filer's identity to any Korean government body, institution, or corporate entity — including through diplomatic channels — could expose the filer and associated survivors to criminal liability in Korea. We ask that this request be flagged to the ATIP coordinator handling reference EA2025_0134154.

We can provide .eml source files for verification of all outreach to the Embassy. The Embassy can verify its own server records.

A structural note on why this is not surprising — and why it should concern Ministers Anand and McGuinty directly. Global Affairs Canada's own parliamentary briefing document (November 20, 2024, tabled before the Standing Committee on Foreign Affairs and International Development) acknowledges that Canada's diplomatic corps does not reflect the diversity of the country it represents — including documented underrepresentation of racialized communities in senior and ambassadorial roles. Canada's most senior diplomatic presence in Seoul — the country now proposing to become Canada's primary defense industrial partner — has historically been staffed without the racialized linguistic and cultural competence necessary to evaluate the concerns Gender Watchdog raised: racialized sexual violence targeting Chinese, Vietnamese, and Zainichi Korean students; institutional cover-up operating through Korean-language defamation law; academic fraud documented in Korean institutional databases.

GAC briefing — "Diversity and Inclusion in the Foreign Service" (Nov 20, 2024, AEFA): https://international.canada.ca/en/global-affairs/corporate/transparency/briefing-documents/parliamentary-committee/2024-11-20-aefa

Subsequent to June 2025, the Canadian Embassy in Seoul has been included in all major advocacy distribution lists:Embassy update on Dongguk's "panic scrub" deletion of UBC (Jan 24, 2026)ATIXA and G7 embassy outreach on 34 confirmed falsified university partnerships (Jan 8, 2026)January 26, 2026 first Hanwha procurement warning

February 9, 2026 — A Canadian constituent formally escalated these findings to Vancouver Granville MP Taleeb Noormohamed and MLA Sunita Dhir, explicitly warning of the Hanwha submarine deal risk and the "Prestige Gap" consumer fraud affecting international students. This established a direct domestic parliamentary record of the evidence.

By February 27, 2026 — the date of this letter — the Canadian Embassy Seoul and domestic parliamentary offices have received documented evidence across ten months and multiple separate submissions. The ATIP record confirms the embassy has been an information dead end, not a gateway to internal government review.

This is the backdrop against which Minister McGuinty's "fair and objective process" pledge was made.I. What Was Signed on February 25 — and What Was Not

Canada and Korea signed an Agreement on the Protection of Military and Defense Classified Information at the same session in which Korea's defense officials were actively lobbying for the submarine contract.

This agreement creates a secure framework for classified intelligence exchange — before the procurement evaluation has concluded.

The agreement was signed without any publicly disclosed conditionality regarding:ESG compliance or independent labor auditing at Hanwha Ocean's production facilitiesSupply chain due diligence under Canada's Fighting Against Forced Labour and Child Labour in Supply Chains Act (S.C. 2023, c. 9)Independent technical certification of the bidder's Arctic performance claimsHuman rights conditionality consistent with Canada's stated foreign policy commitments

Source — Korea Times, "S. Korea stresses capabilities; Canada pledges 'fair' process" (Feb 26, 2026): https://www.koreatimes.co.kr/world/diplomacy/20260226/s-korea-stresses-capabilities-canada-pledges-fair-processII. "Fair for Whom?" — Three Dimensions the Process Has Not AddressedA. Workers at the Geoje Shipyard Canada Already Visited

On February 2, 2026, Secretary of State Fuhr toured Hanwha Ocean's Geoje shipyard (Okpo facility) — the primary production site proposed for the submarine contract.

Days after that visit, unionized workers at the same facility publicly exposed a discriminatory two-tier wage structure: 4,000 migrant subcontracted workers had received year-end bonuses equal to 46.8% of what Korean subcontracted workers at the same site received. This is not an allegation — it was documented by the workers' union and reported by Korea Times.

South Korean President Lee Jae-myung had publicly praised the arrangement as equal treatment in his December 2025 address. The company's own public promise of equalization had not been honored.

Source — Korea Times, "Hanwha Ocean's conflict with subcontracted workers undermines Lee's praise" (Feb 20, 2026): https://www.koreatimes.co.kr/business/companies/20260220/hanwha-oceans-conflict-with-subcontracted-workers-undermines-lees-praise

Canada's Fighting Against Forced Labour and Child Labour in Supply Chains Act (S.C. 2023, c. 9) applies to this supply chain. The Act — in force as of January 1, 2024 — requires entities doing business in Canada to report annually on steps taken to prevent and reduce the risk of forced labour and child labour throughout their supply chains, including through contracts with government entities. Failure to comply carries penalties of up to $250,000 CAD. No public disclosure of supply chain due diligence has been reported in connection with Secretary of State Fuhr's February 2 site visit or the procurement evaluation process.

Source — Fighting Against Forced Labour and Child Labour in Supply Chains Act, S.C. 2023, c. 9 (full text, Justice Canada): https://laws-lois.justice.gc.ca/eng/acts/F-10.6/

We raised this directly with Secretary of State Fuhr, Mohawk College, Ontario Shipyards, the NDDN Committee, and media on February 24, 2026 — one day before the ministerial meetings. No response has been received.

Full documentation: https://blog.genderwatchdog.org/blinded-by-white-how-koreas-racial-playbook-is-selling-canada-a-60b-ghost-ship/B. The Unaudited "200,000 Jobs" Claim

At the February 25 joint press conference, Korea's Foreign Minister Cho Hyun stated that the Hanwha submarine contract would generate "200,000 jobs over 15 years" for Canada.

This figure originates from Hanwha's own lobbying materials. It is derived from a KPMG study commissioned by Hanwha and published as sponsored content in The Hill Times on January 22, 2026. The study's author, Stéphanie Tremblay (Senior Manager, Strategy and Economics at KPMG), explicitly qualified the findings: "these figures represent high-level estimates based on preliminary information provided by Hanwha and are expected to evolve." Furthermore, the projection refers to "job-years" — meaning approximately 15,000 annual jobs, not 200,000 permanent positions. It has not been independently audited by any Canadian institution.

Source — Hill Times (Sponsored Content), "200,000 job-years of sustained employment: Why Hanwha’s submarine bid is an economic engine for Canada" (Jan 22, 2026): https://www.hilltimes.com/sponsored/200000-job-years-of-sustained-employment-why-hanwhas-submarine-bid-is-an-economic-engine-for-canada/

The "technology transfer" component underpinning the jobs projection — specifically, the Korean academic engineering pipeline Hanwha has cited as its talent source — is currently in documented crisis.

Seoul National University, Korea's flagship engineering institution, took its international partner database offline during the very week Canada's defense delegation visited Korea in early February 2026 — an action consistent with the pattern of evidence suppression that Gender Watchdog began documenting in January 2026.

Source — Gender Watchdog Blog, "The Harvard of Korea Has Pulled the Plug: SNU Goes Dark" (Jan 26, 2026): https://blog.genderwatchdog.org/the-harvard-of-korea-has-pulled-the-plug-snu-goes-dark/

Dongguk University — the institution most directly linked to Hanwha's academic partnership ecosystem — was confirmed to have fabricated a partnership with the University of British Columbia. UBC confirmed in writing, following intervention by BC's Office of the Information and Privacy Commissioner, that no such institutional agreement had ever been executed. Dongguk then silently removed UBC from its database while simultaneously reverting Toronto Metropolitan University to its pre-2022 name, "Ryerson University" — forensic evidence of a botched record purge, not clerical error.

Source — Gender Watchdog Blog, "Panic Scrub: Dongguk Deletes UBC, Reverts to Dead Names" (Jan 19, 2026): https://blog.genderwatchdog.org/panic-scrub-dongguk-deletes-ubc-reverts-to-dead-names/

Source — Gender Watchdog Blog, "Semantic Fraud: How Dongguk University's Global Network Collapsed" (full forensic report): https://blog.genderwatchdog.org/semantic-fraud-how-dongguk-universitys-global-network-collapsed-34-fake-partners-exposed/

A university system that falsifies international partnerships to inflate rankings will falsify technology transfer capability to win defense contracts. An independent audit of Hanwha's cited academic infrastructure is a prerequisite of evaluating the "200,000 jobs" claim on its merits.

The fraud is not contained to Dongguk University. Seoul National University (SNU) — routinely described as Korea's Harvard and the stated crown jewel of Hanwha's academic talent pipeline — has exhibited the same pattern. SNU's international partner database was taken offline during the precise week Canada's defense delegation visited Korea in late January–early February 2026, then restored under a newly rebranded domain ("Office of International Affairs" → "Office of Global Affairs") — while a confirmed falsified partnership with Ritsumeikan University remained in the live database. SNU did not correct the fraudulent entry. It changed the sign on the door.

Source — Gender Watchdog Blog, "The Harvard of Korea Has Pulled the Plug: SNU Goes Dark" (Jan 26, 2026, with Updates Jan 28–Feb 5, 2026): https://blog.genderwatchdog.org/the-harvard-of-korea-has-pulled-the-plug-snu-goes-dark/

Gender Watchdog's audit of Korea's university partnership fraud is ongoing and expanding. We are conducting outreach to the world's top 1,000 universities and to international legal clinics to pursue accountability through litigation against ranking agencies — including QS — that have perpetuated and profited from this fraud. Professor John Ioannidis of Stanford University, in direct correspondence with Gender Watchdog, acknowledged that some institutions have become "addicted to gaming" rankings systems. Our forensic documentation is being shared widely. As each new fabricated partnership is confirmed, the evidentiary foundation for that litigation strengthens — and with it, the case that the entire Korean academic pipeline underpinning Hanwha's technology transfer claims is structurally compromised.

The human stakes of this academic fraud are documented and concrete: Tu Anh, a 25-year-old Vietnamese graduate of Keimyung University, fell to her death in October 2025 fleeing an immigration raid at a car parts factory in Daegu after the international degree she was marketed produced no viable visa pathway — the first documented case of lethal consumer fraud in the Korean higher education market.

Source — Hankyoreh English, "[Column] Tu Anh deserved better than to die in fear of immigration raid" (Dec 12, 2025): https://english.hani.co.kr/arti/english_edition/english_editorials/1234344.html

Source — Gender Watchdog Blog, "The 'Panic Scrub': Dongguk University Deletes UBC Partners, Reverts to 'Dead Names' in Failed Cover-Up — including Ioannidis correspondence" (Jan 19, 2026): https://blog.genderwatchdog.org/panic-scrub-dongguk-deletes-ubc-reverts-to-dead-names/C. The Independence of the Evaluation Process

Minister McGuinty stated Canada has established a "whole new process" to conduct the evaluation fairly and at arm's length.

Two independent technical experts — one Western, one Korean — have both concluded that this process has been structurally compromised before any evaluation has been completed.

Ret. Korean Navy Capt. Moon Keun-sik warned in February 2026 that the evaluation structure dangerously underweights technical performance. The official bid instructions confirmed by CBC News reveal that technical platform performance accounts for only 20% of the score, with the remaining 80% weighted toward sustainment (50%), economic benefits (15%), and financial capability (15%). This structure validates Capt. Moon's concern: the factor that determines whether Canadian submariners survive an Arctic emergency is a minority component of the decision.

He wrote: "Submarine weapon systems are directly tied to the lives of their crews. Escape and rescue options for submarines are extremely limited."

Source — Korea Times, "What's Lost in Canada's Bid to Purchase Naval Vessels" (Ret. Capt. Moon Keun-sik, Feb 16, 2026): https://www.koreatimes.co.kr/opinion/columns/guestcolumns/20260216/whats-lost-in-canadas-bid-to-purchase-naval-vessels

Source — CBC News, "Canada’s instructions to submarine contract bidders highlight sustainment, economic benefits" (Nov 20, 2025): https://www.cbc.ca/news/politics/military-submarine-contract-bidders-9.6985543

Dr. Andrew Latham (Professor of International Relations, Macalester College; Non-resident Fellow, Defense Priorities) reached the same conclusion in August 2025 — six months before Hanwha began its Ontario lobbying tour. His analysis: the KSS-III is "optimized for someone else's war." The submarine was designed for Korea's shallow Northeast Asian theater against North Korea. Canada's Arctic Archipelago requires a fundamentally different platform. Korea has never exported the KSS-III. Canada would be the first foreign test case — absorbing all integration risk on a platform with no NATO pedigree and no allied supply chain. The German Type 212CD, by contrast, is operational across four NATO navies with a battle-tested hydrogen fuel-cell AIP system proven to keep submarines silently submerged for up to three weeks.

Source — National Security Journal, "Canada Must Go German For Its New Submarine" (Dr. Andrew Latham, Aug 26, 2025): https://nationalsecurityjournal.org/canada-must-go-german-for-its-new-submarine/

Korea's own defense export record further undermines the "on time and within budget" claim made at the February 25 press conference. Poland — one of Korea's closest defense partners — rejected Hanwha's submarine bid in favor of Sweden's Saab, determining the Korean model was ill-suited for Baltic Sea conditions.

Source — Korea JoongAng Daily, "Poland Submarine Bid Loss Offers Lessons for Korea's Defense Industry" (Dec 30, 2025): https://koreajoongangdaily.joins.com/news/2025-12-30/opinion/columns/Poland-submarine-bid-loss-offers-lessons-for-Koreas-defense-industry/2488520III. Prior Correspondence — Documented Institutional Receipt

The full diplomatic and governmental record is set out in Section 0 above. For completeness, the three recent ministerial-level submissions are:

January 26, 2026: Warning submitted to the Prime Minister's Office, DND, Global Affairs Canada Seoul, and the NDDN Committee covering Korea's academic talent pipeline crisis, semantic fraud in university partnerships, and the AIP technology transfer risk.

February 24, 2026: Evidence submitted directly to Secretary of State Fuhr, Mohawk College, Ontario Shipyards, opposition critics, NDDN Committee, and national media — covering the Geoje wage discrimination scandal, Forced Labour Act obligations, and the full technical record compiled by Latham and Capt. Moon.

Gender Watchdog's documentation has produced a 10x institutional traffic spike within 48 hours of prior submissions, consistent with systematic review by decision-making bodies. A traffic spike of 129 views in a single day was recorded following our August 2025 first warning — up from a baseline of 2–15 daily views.

Source — Gender Watchdog Blog, "Traffic Spike Evidence: Pattern of Institutional Monitoring Across Arms Export Campaigns" (Oct 29, 2025): https://blog.genderwatchdog.org/traffic-spike-evidence-institutional-monitoring-arms-export-campaigns/

The evidence has reached the decision-makers. The record has been established. What has not occurred is a formal response, a commissioned audit, or a public statement that ESG compliance and supply chain due diligence form part of what Minister McGuinty called the "fair and objective" evaluation.IV. The Exploitation Economy Canada Is Being Asked to Enter

The procurement and co-production relationship Canada is developing with Korea is not an isolated defense contract. It is entry into an institutional environment that the Korean government's own research body and international human rights organizations have documented as operating a systematic exploitation economy — one that targets foreign nationals, weaponizes legal systems against survivors, and exports its demand regionally.

The evidence base is not Gender Watchdog's alone. It includes a Korean government-funded institute (KWDI), Human Rights Watch, and direct victim testimony collected on China's Xiaohongshu platform — testimony that broke through despite active censorship pressure. The framing as potential crimes against humanity is drawn by analogy from a parallel UN-level finding: in February 2026, UN Human Rights Council experts assessed that the Epstein network's conduct may meet the Rome Statute threshold for crimes against humanity. We argue the structural architecture in Korea — documented 61.5% sexual violence rate, non-consent rape law, weaponized criminal defamation — satisfies the same definitional elements independently.

The institutional capture is not limited to universities. Three additional exhibits establish how deep it runs:

Exhibit A — The Vice Justice Minister. Former Vice Justice Minister Kim Hak-ui was summoned by prosecutors in May 2019 over allegations of receiving sexual services and kickbacks from a building contractor, including rape allegations from a woman at private sex parties held for ranking officials. Despite being cleared in 2013 investigations, fresh bribery and abuse of power charges were pursued after a justice ministry fact-finding panel recommended reopening the case. His departure was stopped at the airport as he attempted to leave the country under a secretly issued travel ban.

Source — Korea Times, "Former Vice Justice Minister Summoned Over Sex/Bribery Scandal" (May 9, 2019): https://www.koreatimes.co.kr/southkorea/law-crime/20190509/former-vice-justice-minister-summoned-over-sexbribery-scandal-photos

Exhibit B — The Judiciary. In May 2025, the Democratic Party of Korea released photographs showing Judge Jee Kui-youn — the presiding judge in former President Yoon Suk-yeol's insurrection trial — at a high-end hostess bar in Gangnam where, per the party's account, "female workers are expected to join customers in their rooms." The judge had previously denied ever having visited such an establishment. The Supreme Court's inspector general was subsequently expected to investigate. The companions pictured with the judge were identified as having ties to the legal profession.

Source — Hankyoreh English, "Democrats Release Photos of Judge's Alleged Visit to Hostess Bar" (May 20, 2025): https://english.hani.co.kr/arti/english_edition/e_national/1198459.html

Exhibit C — The President and Prosecution Capture. Current President Lee Jae-myung faces approximately 12 outstanding criminal charges across multiple cases. In November 2025, the prosecution waived its appeal in the Daejang-dong land development corruption case linked to Lee's tenure as Seongnam mayor — a decision that prevented recovery of more than 781 billion won (~$533M CAD) in criminal proceeds. Chief district prosecutors nationwide demanded the acting prosecutor general's resignation. Korea Herald's editorial board described prosecutors who did not dare to submit an appeal as motivated by career self-preservation: "They likely feared a career setback if they processed a case related to President Lee based on their conviction."

Source — Korea Herald, "Resolve Lost" (editorial, Nov 13, 2025): https://www.koreaherald.com/article/10614855

Exhibit D — Direct Victim Testimony. A Gender Watchdog post on Xiaohongshu (Little Red Book) about Dongguk University's sexual violence crisis achieved 3,256 views and 85 comments, with multiple victims breaking their silence in the comment threads — including a graduate school applicant who withdrew her admission after a Dongguk professor asked inappropriate personal questions during her interview, and a student who reported that a foreign film student was sexually assaulted by a senior student the previous semester. One theatre student initially posted about "the silent tears and loneliness of nobodies" behind feminist discussions at the university — then edited the comment to remove all references to pain and suffering, demonstrating the climate of institutional fear.

Source — Gender Watchdog Blog, "Viral Xiaohongshu Post Exposes Dongguk University Sexual Violence Crisis": https://blog.genderwatchdog.org/viral-xiaohongshu-post-exposes-dongguk-university-sexual-violence-crisis-victims-break-their-silence/

Canada's bilateral obligations under the Feb 25 classified information agreement and the CMPA-PGK co-production MOU (December 17, 2025) together represent the deepest institutional entanglement with this ecosystem that Canada has yet undertaken.A. The Statistical Baseline: 61.5% Sexual Violence Rate

The Korean Women's Development Institute (KWDI) — a Korean government-linked research body — published a 2020 survey documenting a 61.5% rate of sexual violence among female students in university arts and culture programs. Film departments scored the highest risk rating (81/100) of all academic disciplines. Korea Times' reporting of the same data places 66% of all campus sexual violence across disciplines at the hands of male-dominated faculty.

This is the talent pipeline underpinning Hanwha's "200,000 jobs" projection and the CMPA's new co-production framework.

Source — KWDI report context: https://blog.genderwatchdog.org/sexual-violence-in-arts-education-after-me-too-current-status-and-policy-issues/B. The Legal Architecture That Enables It

Rape law without consent standard. Article 297 of South Korea's Penal Code defines rape as intercourse by means of "violence or intimidation" — not absence of consent. Courts have further narrowed this to require that the violence render the victim unable to resist. A legislative proposal to adopt a consent-based standard was introduced, advanced — and then cancelled in February 2023 under male-backlash pressure.

Source: Human Rights Watch, "South Korea Cancels Plans to Update Definition of Rape" (Feb 1, 2023): https://www.hrw.org/news/2023/02/01/south-korea-cancels-plans-update-definition-rape

Criminal defamation law weaponized against survivors. Korean criminal defamation law allows prosecution for true statements that damage reputation. This is not a gap — it is the enforcement mechanism that keeps the 61.5% violence rate unreported and unredressed. Article 19 has documented how these provisions constitute a "grave threat to freedom of expression," and the Korea Economic Institute (KEI) has noted that "truth alone is accepted as a complete defense" in democracies like the US and Canada, but not in Korea, where even true statements must serve a "public interest" test that protects the powerful.

When Gender Watchdog documented the Dongguk-Tcha Seung-jai case (see Section IV.E below), Sidus FNH retained law firm Shinwon (신원) and issued a criminal defamation threat against our advocacy. The threat was eventually withdrawn, but the chilling effect on other potential witnesses is documented.

Sources:Article 19, "South Korea: Criminal defamation provisions threaten freedom of expression" (May 2018): https://www.article19.org/resources/south-korea-repressive-criminal-defamation-provisions-threaten-freedom-of-expression/Korea Economic Institute, "Problems with Korea's Defamation Law" (Jan 2019): https://keia.org/the-peninsula/problems-with-koreas-defamation-law/Sidus legal threat analysis: https://blog.genderwatchdog.org/sidus-legal-threat-backfires-evidence-of-corporate-panic-and-institutional-cover-up-at-dongguk-university/

These three elements — a 61.5% structural sexual violence rate, a rape law requiring proof of inability to resist, and criminal prosecution for true statements — constitute the basis for the legal argument we are advancing to the UN Human Rights Council: that this structure meets the Rome Statute threshold for crimes against humanity. This is a significant legal escalation. While not yet a UN finding regarding Korea, we argue that the documented institutional conditions satisfy the criteria for a "widespread and systematic attack against a civilian population pursuant to organizational policy" by analogy to the Epstein panel's recent language. UN HRC experts issued an assessment in February 2026 that the Epstein network may meet the "crimes against humanity" threshold — a "global criminal enterprise" operating through "supremacist beliefs, racism, corruption and extreme misogyny." We submit that the same analysis applies here.

