The Save My Seoul documentary (2017, CIVL-TV) provides direct, on-camera testimony from Korean corporate culture insiders:24:40 — "In Korea...prostitution is culture"27:28 — "8 out of 10 Korean males have paid for sex"30:20 — "Hosting (sexual entertainment)" described as a normalized corporate expectation30:33 — "Corporate slush fund to use on prostitution" — systematic corporate financing of sex trafficking operations, documented as standard business entertainment accounting40:45 — Police officer, on prostituted women including minors: "you can't call them victims"43:47 — Korean government built a state-sponsored brothel network near US military bases in the 1960s; women were registered and issued health certificates by the state

The Korean government Ministry for Gender Equality acknowledged the scale by offering cash incentives in 2006 to companies whose male employees refrained from purchasing sex at office parties — an explicit recognition that this was "an ingrained part of Korean corporate culture."

The historical precedent for state involvement is undeniable. During the 1960s, prostitution and related businesses generated nearly 25% of South Korean GNP (Source: Katharine H.S. Moon, Sex Among Allies: Military Prostitution in U.S.-Korea Relations). While official estimates later decreased to ~1.6–4% of GDP in the 2000s, the state's liability for facilitating sexual violence against women servicing the U.S. military was confirmed by the Supreme Court of Korea in September 2022, which ordered the government to compensate victims for the state's active role in managing and encouraging the system.

US State Department Trafficking in Persons Reports repeatedly identify Korean men as primary consumers of child sex tourism in Vietnam, Cambodia, Mongolia, and the Philippines. The demand embedded in Korean corporate culture does not remain within Korea's borders.

UN CEDAW documentation. The UN Committee on the Elimination of All Forms of Discrimination Against Women has formally documented Korean authorities' structural failure to identify and protect Filipina women trafficked into Korea through entertainment-industry recruitment pipelines, establishing that the exploitation network operates transnationally and that Korean state institutions have failed to disrupt it.

