Canada's Fighting Against Forced Labour and Child Labour in Supply Chains Act (S.C. 2023, c. 9) requires reporting entities to identify risks across their supply chains. An industrial cooperation agreement the scale of the submarine contract — involving sustained corporate hospitality, business travel, and personnel exchange — is not exempt from the cultural and legal environment in which it operates.D. Transatlantic National Security Risk: The DOJ Indictment

The exploitation economy is not just a human rights issue; it is a documented counter-intelligence vulnerability. In November 2023, three individuals (including South Korean nationals) were federally indicted in the United States for operating a high-end brothel network in Massachusetts and the Northern Virginia suburbs of Washington, D.C.

The U.S. Department of Justice explicitly noted that the network's clientele included military officers and government contractors with security clearances — the exact demographic that evaluates defense procurement bids. Acting U.S. Attorney Josh Levy stated on the record that the network "could raise national security concerns about the potential for blackmail."

If South Korean nationals are operating exploitation networks targeting security-cleared defense contractors and military officers in the United States, the exact same counter-intelligence and blackmail risks apply to Canadian defense contractors, military officers, and procurement officials evaluating a $60 billion submarine bid. This directly challenges the integrity of the "arm's length and objective" evaluation process.

Source — CNN, "High-end brothel network arrests" (Nov 8, 2023): https://www.cnn.com/2023/11/08/politics/high-end-brothel-network-arrests/index.htmlE. The Canadian Film Industry Pipeline: CMPA Already Inside This Ecosystem

On December 17, 2025 — two months before the February 25 Ottawa ministerial meetings — the Canadian Media Producers Association (CMPA) signed a Memorandum of Understanding with the Producers Guild of Korea (PGK).

Gender Watchdog notified CMPA President Reynolds Mastin and Board Chair Kyle Irving (Eagle Vision) in writing on February 18, 2026 of the documented risks in this partnership. As of February 27, 2026, no substantive response addressing the documented risk factors has been received.

The specific risk we documented for CMPA is directly relevant to your offices:

Tcha Seung-jai (차승재) — three-term President of the Korean Film Producers Association (KFPA) and founder of Sidus FNH — was a professor at Dongguk University's Graduate School of Digital Image & Contents when he was indicted in June 2015 for embezzling approximately 350 million KRW (~CAD $340,000) from government education subsidies. In his 2017 trial, he was acquitted of embezzlement but convicted of bid-rigging — manipulating which vendors received contracts funded by public education money.

Dongguk University's institutional response: they promoted him — appointing him Research Institute Director in 2020, then elevating him to Dean of the Film & Video Production Department in 2023, six years after his criminal conviction.

This is not an isolated case. It is the model. A man convicted of defrauding public education subsidies was elevated to the highest academic position in the department whose students feed directly into the same Korean entertainment industry Canada's film producers are now partnered with. The same pattern — institutional promotion of individuals convicted or credibly accused of exploiting public trust — runs through Dongguk's falsification of 34+ international university partnerships.

A university that falsifies academic partnerships falsifies technology transfer capability. A university that promotes convicted bid-riggers to deanships will protect a defense contractor from supply chain scrutiny. These are the same institutional reflexes, operating at different scales.

